Sunday School Curriculum — Core Standards

Always-loaded reference. Kept deliberately short: only what's needed on nearly every task, regardless of which Sunday or which piece. Everything else, the full year map, workflow, piece anatomy, catechism and Scripture text, lives in Google Drive, "00 Program Documents," and is pulled in on demand. See 00-Program-Index.md there for the full map. Design and typography no longer live here at all; see §5.

Last revised September 7, 2026. The five Level Questions are replaced by a cumulative ladder and the governing Christ sentence is added to §1. Moralism and therapeutic deism are stated as corruptions of things this curriculum teaches, rather than named and left as bare negations. Nursery is given a positive direction. Level labels for Middle School and High School now carry their grade bands.

1. The Governing Idea

Each lesson holds before us our Lord Jesus Christ. His words and his works, given for you, are the object of every lesson at every level.

The whole congregation hears one Gospel each Sunday. Every level studies that same Gospel, at its own depth, the same week it's preached at 8:00 and 11:00. Nothing is thematically freelanced.

The lectionary is the syllabus. Texts are not chosen for being kid-friendly; the appointed pericope is taught honestly at every age, repetition and all.
Every lesson preaches Christ, not the hero of the story. The default failure mode is moralism, David was brave, so be brave. This curriculum does not do that. But the cure is not an hour with nothing in it to do. A text that commands is not the problem, and the Small Catechism is more than half imperative. If the text is about thankfulness, the lesson teaches thankfulness. What is refused is the imperative displacing the gift. The "so what" never becomes try harder as the terms of standing before God; it lands on this is what has been done for you, and then on vocation, which is what the gift does next.
The child is addressed as a baptized saint, not a prospect. No decision theology, no "ask Jesus into your heart." The Law is preached in earnest; the Gospel is given, not offered as a transaction.
2. Confessional Standards
Standard	Decision
Doctrinal norm	Book of Concord (1580), quia subscription
Catechism	Luther's Small Catechism with Explanation, CPH 2017
Hymnal	Lutheran Service Book (2006)
Lectionary	LSB Historic One-Year Series
Church body	LCMS
Scripture translation	ESV, effective Trinity 16 (Sept 20, 2026) forward. Rally Day / Trinity 15 (Sept 13) is complete in NKJV and stays that way, a grandfathered historical exception, not the standard.
Lord's Prayer	"Forgive us our trespasses", always, regardless of translation. Prayed together, never printed.
Benediction	Apostolic Benediction (2 Corinthians 13:14), printed in full, last in the Closing. Every piece except the Family Take-Home.

Scripture is the source of the confession. The Creeds and the Confessions come alongside Scripture as the Church's careful statement of it, never beside it as a second authority. Where a lesson sets the catechism next to the text, the ordering is stated, not assumed.

Anti-patterns explicitly refused: moralism, decision theology, Bible-hero worship, therapeutic deism, allegorizing away the plain sense, sentimentality in place of the Law's actual sting.

Two of those are corruptions of things this curriculum teaches, and naming only the corruption has made writers flinch from the good thing itself.

Moralism is a corruption of the Law. The Law in all three of its offices, curb, mirror, and guide, is God's description of what is good, so it always yields understanding alongside whatever else it is doing. Wisdom tempers the three uses; it is not a fourth use, and wisdom here means God's description of the good, not prudence and not what works out well. Moralism is the imperative displacing the gift. It fails in two distinct ways: the demand eats the hour and the child leaves under Law, which is common and a matter of proportion and order; or the demand becomes the terms of his standing before God, which is rarer and worse.

Therapeutic deism is a corruption of the Gospel. The Gospel is news with content before it is an effect. Christ's history is given first, and the comfort follows from it necessarily. The classroom task is not to extract comfort from a text but to put students in front of the Christ the text reports on. If a lesson's Gospel could be preached had nothing happened outside Jerusalem in the year 33, it is not the Gospel.

3. The Levels

Six printed labels. Five of them are rungs on one ladder; Nursery stands before it.

Level labels are printed text. They appear verbatim in the chip at the head of every piece, so they are written exactly as follows and nowhere varied: Pre-K / K, Primary 1–2, Intermediate 3–5, Middle School 6–8, High School 9–12, Nursery. Grade bands carry no parentheses. (The earlier parenthetical form, Primary (1–2), is retired.) The grade bands on Middle School and High School are effective Trinity 16 forward; Rally Day / Trinity 15 printed those two labels bare and is not being reprinted.

Levels are named by grade band, never by number, which avoids collision with the congregation's Learn-by-Heart memory program.

The Ladder

What differs by level is the question asked of the text, not the topic. The ladder is cumulative: each level asks its own rung and every rung beneath it.

Level	The Rung
Pre-K / K	What does the text say?
Primary 1–2	What does this mean?
Intermediate 3–5	What does it demand, and what does it give?
Middle School 6–8	What does this text claim, and how does the Lord answer those who deny it?
High School 9–12	Who denies this, and how is it confessed to them?

The rungs are fixed constants. They do not vary by week and are not rewritten per Sunday. They were amended once, deliberately, on September 7, 2026, when the five earlier Level Questions were replaced. Rung 1 replaces What happened? because roughly a third of the one-year lectionary is not narrative, and what happened forces discourse texts and parables into a story shape they do not have.

Nursery

Not a rung, and not less than the others. Care for the children. Tell them about Jesus in the simplest words. Sing the simple hymns over and over, so that they learn the sound of the Good Shepherd's voice (John 10). This replaces the earlier direction, presence, not instruction. Nursery still gets no lesson and no student piece, but the telling and the singing are deliberate, and the week's simple sentence about Jesus is carried on the Nursery Notes.

The Trivium
Level	Art
Pre-K / K	Grammar, first form
Primary 1–2	Grammar, second form
Intermediate 3–5	Grammar completing, logic beginning
Middle School 6–8	Logic
High School 9–12	Rhetoric

The art names the mode of instruction, never the object. The object of the hour is receptive at all five levels. Without this constraint the logic stage seats the student as judge over the text, which is the error this scheme was written to remove.

Session Length

Standard session length is 60 minutes, every level, effective September 4, 2026. Because it is a fixed constant rather than something that varies by level, individual Teacher's Guides do not state it as a standing line. See 06-Piece-Specifications.md in Drive for how exceptions appear on the page.

Rally Day (Sept 13, 2026) is a documented one-time exception, 40 minutes, shortened for the all-ages opening, not a change to the standard above.

4. Standing Production Parameters

These apply to every Sunday unless a specific week overrides them. (Full piece anatomy: 06-Piece-Specifications.md in Drive. Full workflow: 05-Weekly-Production-Workflow.md in Drive.)

Production ends at a text handoff. Each week produces one Markdown file, structured to the Week Template, covering all twelve pieces in manifest order. Design and typesetting happen in the design project, which owns every visual decision. Finished .docx and PDF files are no longer produced here. That same handoff file is the source for Base44 when the app is built, so there is one text and one structure feeding both.

The twelve pieces, in manifest order: Family Take-Home, Nursery Notes, then Teacher's Guide and student piece for each of the five instructional levels. The Pre-K / K student piece is the Craft Page; the other four are Student Handouts.

Theme Line: every one of the 12 pieces for a Sunday carries the same one-sentence theme, identical wording across all 12. Effective Trinity 16 forward. Approved and locked before production.
Title: a short h1 carried on all 12 pieces, distinct from the Theme Line. Approved and locked alongside it.
Art program: Schnorr von Carolsfeld woodcuts where a plate exists; generated art fills gaps. No stained glass photography.
Attribution: teacher-facing pieces carry "These Sunday School Lessons are built especially for St. Paul Lutheran Church by Pastor Bryan Wolfmueller with Claude AI on the same Sunday Gospel Lesson." Student handouts carry none.
Catechism section heading: "From the Small Catechism", used identically on every Teacher's Guide and every Student Handout, all levels.
Nothing internal on the page. Anything written into the handoff may be printed and put in a volunteer's hand. No section references, no production notes, no DRAFT markers surviving to handoff.
